From the interface batch 2 (2026-10-14), same source:

- Attaching to a stock shared instance as a local client: the abstract socket
  `\0rns/<instance_name>` on Linux and Android (connected off the async
  workers), falling back to TCP, and TCP elsewhere; a 1 Gbit/s autoconfigured
  MTU, ingress control off, an 8 s redial and, 10 s after it, path responses
  for every registered destination. Hops through the instance count none, and
  an announce it re-emits at hops 0 (a sibling client) keeps no transport
  node, since a stock client drops a header-type-2 packet naming the instance.
  Loss is scoped to the lost interface: its links fail and its paths, bridges,
  return paths, pending relays and answers, held announces and tunnels go;
  links still awaiting a proof lapse at their deadline. The RPC port is not
  modelled. RNS `Interfaces/LocalInterface.py` 53-202; `Reticulum.py` 350-362,
  730-745; `Utilities/platformutils.py` 55-61; `Transport.py` 1405-1437,
  1936-1939, 2398-2425, 3690-3709.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/local.rs`, `src/endpoint/transit.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/rebroadcast.rs`, `src/rebroadcast.rs`.
- Tunnel synthesis: a 176-byte signed synthesis (the review's 170 was wrong)
  on every connect of a non-KISS TCP client, queued after the carrier is
  online; its interface hash names `TCPClientInterface[host:port]`, so two
  identical clients share a tunnel. A listener validates it, counting a bad
  one as a protocol violation (RNS counts only an exception), mirrors routes
  per tunnel and restores them on reappearance under RNS's hops and timebase
  test, reading the freshness row's timebase recorded when the tunnel went
  away. Unlike RNS: tables bounded at 256 tunnels and the path-table capacity,
  away tunnels evicted first; a route without a freshness row is never
  restored; restores obey the path-table capacity; the else-branch over an
  undefined packet is not ported; while a tunnel is bound, a route learned
  elsewhere drops its mirrored copy. RNS `Interfaces/TCPInterface.py` 169-180,
  298-299; `Interfaces/Interface.py` 181-183; `Transport.py` 157-158, 362,
  1014-1060, 2463-2473, 2764-2860.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/tunnel.rs`, `src/endpoint/dial.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/announces.rs`.
- Path requests at a transport (`RoutingPolicy::forward_announces`): answered
  from a cache of each route's verified announce, bounded and evicted with the
  path table, through the announce table after 0.4 s (1.9 s on a roaming
  interface), as header type 2 with TRANSPORT propagation and hops one past
  the route; never on the roaming interface the path came from, nor to a
  requestor that is the next hop; any live route counts as answered. Unlike
  RNS: concurrent answers for one destination stack rather than overwrite, a
  relay arriving behind a pending answer waits rather than replacing it, and
  answers obey the routing policy's egress and `outgoing`. The relay cap bypass
  for roaming and boundary interfaces is not ported. RNS `Transport.py`
  765-829, 1489-1521, 2456, 3418-3524.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/discovery.rs`, `src/endpoint/rebroadcast.rs`,
  `src/rebroadcast.rs`, `src/endpoint/paths.rs`.
- Recursive path discovery by the requesting interface's mode (boundary
  searches only boundary and gateway interfaces) or `recursive_prs`, on the
  other online interfaces with the requester's tag and our transport id,
  withheld by egress control or the announce cap; the matching announce goes
  straight back to each requester as a path response. A transport sends the
  three-field request. Unlike RNS: the search timeout is max(15 s, the
  slowest known airtime round trip) and the cap gate applies only where the
  airtime is known; batching runs only on a transport; the tables hold 1024;
  expired searches are ignored on access and swept on the next request.
  Local-client forwarding is not modelled. RNS `Transport.py` 1005-1120,
  1862-1884, 2430-2455, 3279-3325, 3420-3436, 3528-3583;
  `Interfaces/Interface.py` 55-56.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/discovery.rs`, `src/path.rs`, `src/rebroadcast.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/announces.rs`.
- Path-request ingress and egress control per interface: burst detection at
  the new-interface and established rates with hold and a cooldown of 3,
  mirrored into the interface statistics, and egress counted preemptively
  with two samples minimum. Unlike RNS: an interface's age runs from its first
  path request, the burst is evaluated once per request (RNS twice), and rates
  are whole Hz. RNS `Interfaces/Interface.py` 213-250, 315-325, 368-389;
  `Transport.py` 1601, 1858.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/discovery/rates.rs`, `src/endpoint/iface_policy.rs`.
- A gravity re-point is relayed as a newly added path would be, bypassing the
  packet-hash dedup as RNS's filter passes single announces, but is not
  published again. The Node core takes the next-hop mode from the live route
  at release and falls back to the ingress interface when its bounded table
  has evicted the route (RNS would not relay). RNS `Transport.py` 1458-1469,
  1668-1672, 2229-2251, 2297-2395.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/announces.rs`, `src/node/rebroadcast.rs`.
- Four inbound lanes (data 1024, announce 128, path request 128, ingress
  limited 8), sorted at enqueue by packet type and the path-request hash, a
  bursting interface's requests going to the ingress-limited lane; drained in
  strict priority, only the data lane back-pressures a stream reader, each
  full lane drops its own class and counts it. The `qlen_in_*` overrides are
  not exposed; released held announces do not re-enter ingress; the router
  yields cooperatively. RNS `Transport.py` 46-95, 111-114, 141-146, 307-317,
  1796-1912; `Reticulum.py` 716-730; `Interfaces/Interface.py` 296.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/inbound_queue.rs`, `src/endpoint/runtime.rs`.
- Per-interface statistics: rx and tx bytes deframed with the access code
  (stock's txb also counts framing), packets, announce and path-request counts
  and bytes, IFAC and protocol violations, held announces, the announce and
  path-request burst flags, online, mode and frame limit, with a JSON form.
  Unlike RNS: announce and path-request receipts are counted when queued
  rather than after validation and dedup; every path request encoded is
  counted; an oversized frame with a bad access code is a protocol violation;
