`src/artifact.rs` and `src/msgpack.rs` implement the RNS signed-artifact
envelope (`.rsg` / `.rsm`). Its layout was read from Prns
(<https://github.com/KenAKAFrosty/Prns>), a Rust Reticulum
implementation, at commit `72b6b30d27cac910ce20d370e1dc711fe9b95955` (v0.3.4).
Prns is dual-licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; Retinue elects **MIT**.

The Rust in those two files was written for this crate and shares no text with
Prns. What was taken is the structure of the envelope: which fields exist, what
they are named, that the metadata map opens with `signer` and `pubkey`, and
that the signature covers the encoded envelope rather than the message. That is
a description of an RNS wire format, and the vectors proving Retinue reproduces
it were captured independently by running RNS 1.4.2's own `rnid` executable
(`oracle/capture_signed_artifact.py`).

The historical test quoted Prns's 224-byte published constant. That comparison
was retired on September 26; current captures use project-selected inputs with
RNS 1.5.2. The original captures remain in the receipt archive and Git history.
